  "notes": "Means to endure, put up with, or hold back. Covers physical pain, emotional frustration, and self-restraint (e.g., resisting temptation). Highly valued in Japanese culture as a personal virtue.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- {我慢|がまん}する: to endure, be patient\n- {我慢|がまん}できる/できない: can/cannot stand it\n- {我慢|がまん}{強|づよ}い: patient, persevering (adjective)\n- {我慢|がまん}の{限界|げんかい}: the limit of one's patience\n\nUSAGE:\nOften used when someone is bearing discomfort or holding back desires. Children are frequently told to {我慢|がまん}する — it is considered an important life skill.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {忍耐|にんたい}: patience, endurance (more formal/literary)\n- {辛抱|しんぼう}: perseverance (emphasizes long-term endurance)\n- {堪|こら}える: to bear, withstand (verb)",
